Port patch 0020 - the Game Boy cartridge title check no longer depends on four variables being adjacent in memory
Why: with a Game Boy cartridge inserted the Transfer Pak flow ended in "The Transfer Pak is not set properly": GbPak_DetectSupportedCartridgeVariant returned 0
for Pokemon Red. It compares the cartridge title with four 16-byte names by starting at &D_8120DD70 (the YELLOW title) and stepping back by 0x10. The other
three names (RED, GREEN, BLUE) are inside the 0x44-byte array D_8120DD2C, which on the console ends exactly where D_8120DD70 begins (the variable names are
the console addresses). The native linker does not keep them together, so the steps back read unrelated data. The patch keeps the order and contents of the
comparison but takes the names from a small pointer table (D_8120DD2C+0x14, +0x24, +0x34 and D_8120DD70). This is the fifth case of the console's variable
layout being relied on by accident (0017, 0018 and the aliases are the others).

--- a/src/fragments/gb_tower_emulator/gb_tower_emulator_7F9A0.c
+++ b/src/fragments/gb_tower_emulator/gb_tower_emulator_7F9A0.c
@@ -1143,9 sha256=70e0c12eec098b4256a1f0470eb1ec63e30b4fd61c3a80e64d44ace46b3ebef5 @@
=3
-2
+  /* PORT: the four 16-byte titles ("POKEMON RED", "POKEMON GREEN", "POKEMON BLUE", "POKEMON YELLOW"). The decomp walks them backwards from
+   * &D_8120DD70 (the YELLOW title, a separate variable) in steps of 0x10, which only works when the console linker places D_8120DD2C
+   * (0x44 bytes, holding the RED, GREEN and BLUE titles at +0x14, +0x24, +0x34) directly in front of D_8120DD70 (0x8120DD2C + 0x44 ==
+   * 0x8120DD70, so the names are the console addresses). The native linker does not keep them adjacent, the loop compared the cartridge
+   * title with unrelated data, and no Game Boy cartridge was accepted ("Transfer Pak is not set properly"). The same four titles, now
+   * reached without relying on the adjacency. */
+  u8* titles[4];
+
+  titles[0] = D_8120DD2C + 0x14;
+  titles[1] = D_8120DD2C + 0x24;
+  titles[2] = D_8120DD2C + 0x34;
+  titles[3] = D_8120DD70;
+  var_s1 = titles[3];
=1
+      var_s1 = titles[i - 1];
=3
@@ -1156,7 sha256=307136a65963afc7d57cfa0a731698b8b0d2f379414c812d2f1f7b27db2b8038 @@
=3
-1
=3
